1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:02,160
And now, to you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:11,960
Once you decide to become a volunteer leader in scouting,

3
00:00:12,380 --> 00:00:15,420
somebody will tell you, well, you know, it's only an hour a week.

4
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:21,160
You soon find out that it can be considerably more.

5
00:00:21,240 --> 00:00:22,320
Well, is that time worth it?

6
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:28,400
Is it really worth all the time that you spend working with a scout troop or a cub pack?

7
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:31,380
I think I have an answer for you today.

8
00:00:32,299 --> 00:00:33,960
Hey, this is podcast number 12.

9
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:10,440
Welcome back to the Scoutmasters podcast.

10
00:01:10,660 --> 00:01:11,440
This is Clark Green.

11
00:01:11,440 --> 00:01:19,160
So, yeah, everybody asks themselves at one point if all the time we're putting into scouting is really going to matter,

12
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:21,140
if it's going to amount to much of anything.

13
00:01:21,860 --> 00:01:23,620
Well, I want you to ask two people.

14
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:30,479
I want you to ask Scoutmaster John Sexton, who retired as a Master Sergeant,

15
00:01:31,479 --> 00:01:35,880
went to a little town called Alberton, Georgia, set up a scout troop.

16
00:01:35,880 --> 00:01:39,600
And one of the young men he worked with, his name was Otha Thornton.

17
00:01:40,179 --> 00:01:45,420
Otha joined the troop and then went on to become an Eagle Scout and become a very skilled leader,

18
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,300
entered Morehouse College and after graduation joined the United States Army

19
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:58,380
and is about to conclude a 21-year career of very illustrious service as a lieutenant colonel.

20
00:01:58,380 --> 00:02:08,139
Now, Colonel Thornton, his leadership skills and the work that he did as a scout laid a foundation for him to serve on the White House communications staff,

21
00:02:08,220 --> 00:02:10,519
which put him in the same room as two presidents,

22
00:02:11,359 --> 00:02:16,500
and led him to a deployment in Iraq where he had a very key leadership position

23
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:22,380
and a stressful and difficult one, as all of them are on deployment,

24
00:02:22,380 --> 00:02:27,340
but managed to find some free time to rededicate back to scouting

25
00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:32,480
in helping the Iraqi nationals reestablish their scout movement.

26
00:02:32,900 --> 00:02:35,079
So he was able to volunteer some of his time there.

27
00:02:36,780 --> 00:02:43,200
So Scoutmaster John Sexton's hour a week has multiplied itself several times over

28
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:47,980
and will continue to go as Lieutenant Colonel Thornton continues to influence lives

29
00:02:47,980 --> 00:02:52,660
and a new job he'll have back at his alma mater at Morehouse College.

30
00:02:53,140 --> 00:03:01,179
I interviewed the colonel just at Fort Meade, and he's preparing to move pending his retirement.

31
00:03:02,100 --> 00:03:04,519
And so you may hear some noise in the background.

32
00:03:04,660 --> 00:03:09,480
I think he said that the fellows were there recarpeting the home that he was in.

33
00:03:09,620 --> 00:03:15,579
But it's a fascinating interview and also contains some very poignant advice for us as scout leaders.

34
00:03:15,580 --> 00:03:19,580
So without further ado, we'll move right to the interview.

35
00:03:19,820 --> 00:03:23,780
You're from a town about the size of the town I live in, Elberton, Georgia.

36
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:27,280
Elberton is about 100 miles from Atlanta, Georgia.

37
00:03:27,420 --> 00:03:32,020
But like you say, just a nice, quaint town, a place to raise kids.

38
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:36,440
I spent my first four years there as a child, and we moved.

39
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:41,660
And then about eighth grade, we came back to Elberton, where I finished off high school.

40
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:43,240
That's where I call my hometown.

41
00:03:43,240 --> 00:03:46,060
You spent some time in scouts in Elberton, did you?

42
00:03:46,380 --> 00:03:46,940
Yes.

43
00:03:47,140 --> 00:03:52,120
By eighth grade, I began to get involved in the Boy Scouts.

44
00:03:52,580 --> 00:04:01,520
And that's where I, again, took part in the troop leadership positions and the service projects, monthly camping.

45
00:04:02,140 --> 00:04:09,480
I had a very great scout master, and I've kept in touch with him over the last 25, 30 years.

46
00:04:09,920 --> 00:04:11,680
As a matter of fact, I went to see him last week.

47
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:11,860
Okay.

48
00:04:12,160 --> 00:04:12,380
Yep.

49
00:04:12,380 --> 00:04:20,620
He was a retired Army Master Sergeant that moved to Elberton and started a troop.

50
00:04:21,160 --> 00:04:22,860
What was your scout master's name?

51
00:04:23,140 --> 00:04:24,220
John Sexton.

52
00:04:25,160 --> 00:04:26,120
John Sexton.

53
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:26,420
Uh-huh.

54
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:28,940
Now, that sounds like a scout master and a master sergeant.

55
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:29,300
Oh, yeah.

56
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:29,680
Oh, yeah.

57
00:04:30,940 --> 00:04:33,220
That sounds like somebody you better listen to.

58
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:33,560
Oh, yeah.

59
00:04:33,640 --> 00:04:34,020
Oh, yeah.

60
00:04:34,020 --> 00:04:41,039
Can you tell me what was so important about John Sexton to you as a young man?

61
00:04:41,039 --> 00:04:44,539
He was almost like to some guys a father figure, you know.

62
00:04:44,719 --> 00:04:49,340
You know, he took a sincere interest in developing young men.

63
00:04:50,099 --> 00:04:55,560
And, again, he was a no-nonsense type scout leader.

64
00:04:55,560 --> 00:05:02,860
Do you recall any specific merit badge or thing you worked on as scouts that you carried forward into adulthood?

65
00:05:02,860 --> 00:05:14,060
Yeah, I would say the series of merit badges that were most important and very critical today are citizenship, the citizenship merit badges, and communications.

66
00:05:14,460 --> 00:05:18,800
Because I actually – now I'm assigned to White House Communications.

67
00:05:19,140 --> 00:05:22,860
Although I was in Iraq, Tim, I've been with White House Communications for three years.

68
00:05:22,860 --> 00:05:31,900
I was a director of human resources for two years, and I also served as a presidential communications officer.

69
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:34,600
So I've actually got to work for two presidents.

70
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:42,200
And just to see the different levels of government and how they work, it's just – it's been great.

71
00:05:42,380 --> 00:05:44,200
You know, citizenship in action.

72
00:05:44,580 --> 00:05:51,360
Now, do you think at the time when you were working on those badges, do you think you had any understanding of what they would mean to you in the future?

73
00:05:51,360 --> 00:06:04,200
It was just interesting to learn about that because when I went to college, my major was actually urban studies, the concentration in management, basically managing cities.

74
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:13,120
So I learned a lot about how groups worked, which, you know, you did requirements on those citizenship merit badges.

75
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:20,760
How states worked with cities and the federal government, all those community-type things.

76
00:06:20,820 --> 00:06:27,060
You had to learn in communications, making presentations to people and building presentations.

77
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:28,680
All those things came in hand.

78
00:06:29,140 --> 00:06:31,800
So you went on to become an Eagle Scout with the truth.

79
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:32,400
That is correct.

80
00:06:32,760 --> 00:06:34,180
Tell me a little bit about that.

81
00:06:34,540 --> 00:06:40,700
You know, I'm asking you these questions because I've got about three Scouts who are pushing that 18th birthday.

82
00:06:40,700 --> 00:06:40,780
Okay.

83
00:06:41,219 --> 00:06:45,840
And I've got one who's scrambling on those last couple of merit badges and things like that.

84
00:06:46,320 --> 00:06:48,719
And I wonder if you remember that time.

85
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:49,180
I don't know.

86
00:06:49,300 --> 00:06:51,099
How old were you when you became an Eagle Scout?

87
00:06:51,300 --> 00:06:53,680
I finished all my requirements at 17.

88
00:06:53,880 --> 00:06:54,300
Nice.

89
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:58,180
Well, you – Mr. Saxon wasn't going to have it any other way.

90
00:06:58,340 --> 00:06:58,820
Absolutely.

91
00:07:01,820 --> 00:07:04,340
Which youth leadership positions did you hold?

92
00:07:04,340 --> 00:07:09,039
Most of my time in the troop, I was a patrol leader or assistant patrol leader.

93
00:07:09,739 --> 00:07:18,940
And I actually served at the state troop as described during the – I think it was 85 Jamboree.

94
00:07:19,099 --> 00:07:23,179
So you went on after becoming an Eagle Scout, went on to Morehouse.

95
00:07:23,419 --> 00:07:25,239
I graduated from Morehouse.

96
00:07:25,359 --> 00:07:29,599
And then I enlisted in the military for Officer Candidate School.

97
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:40,280
And that, I will tell you, when I enlisted in the military, I saw the importance of Eagle Scout because it is very well respected.

98
00:07:40,740 --> 00:07:49,940
And, you know, as we go through the conversation, I can almost, to the assignment, talk to you about the opportunities that Eagle Scout opened up to.

99
00:07:50,200 --> 00:07:54,040
All the way up to work on the President's – on Officer's President.

100
00:07:54,040 --> 00:07:59,780
And now I've heard stories about, you know, you show up at your induction.

101
00:08:00,300 --> 00:08:05,540
The sergeant asks who is an Eagle Scout and puts them right at the head of the group.

102
00:08:06,120 --> 00:08:08,860
Is that – I've heard stories about that.

103
00:08:08,940 --> 00:08:10,280
Is that reasonably accurate?

104
00:08:10,380 --> 00:08:10,800
I can vouch for that.

105
00:08:10,960 --> 00:08:14,080
You automatically get another rank.

106
00:08:14,400 --> 00:08:17,560
And then opportunities open up for you.

107
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:21,240
I was in Hawaii for four years.

108
00:08:21,240 --> 00:08:23,580
I was an infantry officer over there.

109
00:08:23,660 --> 00:08:33,080
So all those outdoor skills and things you learn, orienteering, compass reading, all that stuff was a big help as an infantry officer.

110
00:08:33,440 --> 00:08:42,000
The company command called us in his office and he said, hey, the troop on post, they just had a lot of folks who rotate out and we need some assistance.

111
00:08:42,140 --> 00:08:42,900
Can you all help us out?

112
00:08:42,919 --> 00:08:43,960
And it was like, not a problem.

113
00:08:44,080 --> 00:08:44,560
You got it.

114
00:08:45,140 --> 00:08:47,460
So me and Keith Natick was his name.

115
00:08:47,460 --> 00:08:50,720
And we began, you know, working and we kept things going.

116
00:08:51,280 --> 00:08:58,960
And about four months, five months until the tour, the base commander called us in.

117
00:08:59,660 --> 00:09:03,620
And he said, hey, y'all are doing great work with the scout troops.

118
00:09:03,700 --> 00:09:10,700
He said, you two let me know where you – talk to your wives and let me know where you want to go next assignment, choice of assignment.

119
00:09:10,700 --> 00:09:16,200
From there, I went to Michigan Tech University where I became very, very involved.

120
00:09:16,940 --> 00:09:18,440
My son was a Cub Scout.

121
00:09:18,900 --> 00:09:23,600
And I actually was the day camp director for two years.

122
00:09:23,820 --> 00:09:25,140
I was there for three years.

123
00:09:25,180 --> 00:09:26,620
I ran the camp for two years.

124
00:09:27,200 --> 00:09:29,700
From Michigan, they sent me to Europe.

125
00:09:30,180 --> 00:09:32,720
We were very, very active there.

126
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:37,180
And we actually – my son and I, we traveled around to international camp outs.

127
00:09:37,320 --> 00:09:40,720
We went to Switzerland every year for the Klondike.

128
00:09:41,540 --> 00:09:46,160
We went to the Baden-Powell camp in East England last year there.

129
00:09:46,260 --> 00:09:49,720
So it was a wonderful scouting opportunity.

130
00:09:50,420 --> 00:09:55,700
So when I got back to the States at Fort Meade, we got back involved with the troop here.

131
00:09:56,180 --> 00:09:58,960
And this troop is very, very active also.

132
00:09:58,960 --> 00:10:03,120
And I spent about, you know, a year just helping out where I could.

133
00:10:03,300 --> 00:10:08,320
Then I became the Eagle Scout advisor after my second year here and did that for two years.

134
00:10:08,780 --> 00:10:12,280
I mean, I'm just saying there's so many opportunities like that for Eagle Scouts.

135
00:10:12,280 --> 00:10:14,420
I find this to be a very important thing.

136
00:10:14,640 --> 00:10:20,360
The fellows that become Eagle Scouts, even the guys that have been in the scout troop for several years before they head off to college,

137
00:10:20,980 --> 00:10:24,900
it seems they get to school and they find their feet a lot quicker than some of their peers.

138
00:10:25,040 --> 00:10:25,380
Yes.

139
00:10:25,380 --> 00:10:27,420
The critical skill is that planning.

140
00:10:27,420 --> 00:10:29,920
And, you know, setting goals.

141
00:10:30,819 --> 00:10:36,819
So that's the beautiful thing about earning the Eagle Scout and the skill set that you bring from it.

142
00:10:36,880 --> 00:10:38,319
You identify your goals.

143
00:10:38,380 --> 00:10:39,079
Because I'll give you an example.

144
00:10:39,199 --> 00:10:44,839
In college, again, the Eagle Scouts, it was a couple of my classes like, where's the college catalog?

145
00:10:45,060 --> 00:10:48,319
What is the program to meet the requirements?

146
00:10:48,520 --> 00:10:49,479
And that's what we did.

147
00:10:49,680 --> 00:10:52,219
And we went in and got in and out in four years.

148
00:10:52,220 --> 00:10:56,700
So at one point in your military career, you are attached to the White House.

149
00:10:56,880 --> 00:10:57,700
I'm assigned there.

150
00:10:57,800 --> 00:10:58,900
I'm assigned to White House Communications.

151
00:10:59,500 --> 00:11:02,420
Tell me a little bit about the White House Communications Agency.

152
00:11:02,880 --> 00:11:04,800
They call it the voice of the president.

153
00:11:04,800 --> 00:11:16,120
We basically work with the White House staff and the Secret Service to ensure that the president can communicate instantaneously anywhere in the world.

154
00:11:16,760 --> 00:11:26,160
We basically go ahead of the president, check the communications in the area, and bring whatever necessary equipment we need to ensure that he can communicate.

155
00:11:26,160 --> 00:11:32,160
Sounds like you get to travel a lot and work very closely with the president and his staff.

156
00:11:32,800 --> 00:11:40,420
I've met both presidents, but we protocol for people that work around the president's military.

157
00:11:40,780 --> 00:11:42,839
If they talk to us, we can talk to them.

158
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:43,459
I see.

159
00:11:43,800 --> 00:11:50,620
But again, usually when the president is, whenever you see the president, he has his advisors around and they're talking business.

160
00:11:50,620 --> 00:12:00,220
You went from this little town in Elberton, you became an Eagle Scout, threw Morehouse into the military, ended up in the same room with the president of the United States.

161
00:12:00,540 --> 00:12:01,560
That's pretty remarkable.

162
00:12:02,060 --> 00:12:08,740
My first event I did for President Bush, October 1st, 2007.

163
00:12:09,120 --> 00:12:10,740
And that's where it hit me.

164
00:12:11,080 --> 00:12:16,780
I was standing there with the military aide, with the football, and that's the nuclear case.

165
00:12:16,780 --> 00:12:21,000
And I said, this is a long ways from Elberton, Georgia.

166
00:12:24,400 --> 00:12:29,060
I think everybody that gets in that room has got to be thinking about everything that put them there.

167
00:12:29,480 --> 00:12:29,740
Oh, yeah.

168
00:12:29,880 --> 00:12:30,180
Oh, yeah.

169
00:12:30,360 --> 00:12:34,040
There are a lot of Eagle Scouts in the White House Military Office.

170
00:12:34,540 --> 00:12:41,439
And the reason for that, when we go through the screening process, it's a very stringent selection process.

171
00:12:41,440 --> 00:12:44,920
But once you get it, it's like, wow, you get it.

172
00:12:44,980 --> 00:12:47,520
And then you have to maintain to stay there.

173
00:12:48,220 --> 00:12:54,100
You were the Human Resources Chief of Plans and Operations for the United States Forces in Iraq.

174
00:12:54,320 --> 00:13:00,420
I was basically Senior Vice President of Human Resources, you know, personnel operations.

175
00:13:00,420 --> 00:13:06,060
All essential personnel services, you know, troops getting their mail on time.

176
00:13:06,380 --> 00:13:07,420
Then I had casualty operations.

177
00:13:08,120 --> 00:13:12,660
That was probably the toughest area that I had to run.

178
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:21,459
And basically, from the time a soldier went down, we were responsible for the reporting and tracking until the case was resolved.

179
00:13:21,560 --> 00:13:23,740
So, you got back to the States in the medical system.

180
00:13:23,740 --> 00:13:32,280
Yeah, I can't imagine how stressful and difficult it would be to be responsible for making sure people are informed, I assume.

181
00:13:32,700 --> 00:13:34,180
Yeah, that's zero defect.

182
00:13:34,480 --> 00:13:37,200
You cannot make a mistake.

183
00:13:37,440 --> 00:13:41,000
At least during my watch, there were no errors in that area.

184
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:47,600
And for me, that was full circle because I actually served as a casualty manager.

185
00:13:47,960 --> 00:13:50,180
I actually did casualty notifications.

186
00:13:50,380 --> 00:13:57,740
I had to knock on, you know, five soldiers and doors that were killed in battle.

187
00:13:58,320 --> 00:14:06,060
And I've had to do casualty assistance also, helping families through the process.

188
00:14:06,500 --> 00:14:08,220
That is very, very tough.

189
00:14:08,220 --> 00:14:14,400
You know, you're there with the chaplain in your dress uniform and holding back tears.

190
00:14:14,520 --> 00:14:19,320
And as you see the reaction, again, you have to hold it back.

191
00:14:19,520 --> 00:14:27,360
It does impact you when you think about another human being and, you know, someone's son, someone's daughter.

192
00:14:27,580 --> 00:14:29,740
And it's tough.

193
00:14:30,280 --> 00:14:35,400
Well, you have a very good understanding then of what it is to lay it all on the line.

194
00:14:35,560 --> 00:14:35,940
Oh, yeah.

195
00:14:35,940 --> 00:14:38,560
The real meaning of service in this country then.

196
00:14:38,760 --> 00:14:39,820
I'm very touched by that.

197
00:14:39,920 --> 00:14:40,840
That is really something.

198
00:14:41,500 --> 00:14:50,260
When you were in Iraq, you know, in your free time, I don't know how much free time a person would have when they are in Iraq and have the responsibilities you have.

199
00:14:50,360 --> 00:14:53,700
Somehow you found a way to put your scout leader hat back on.

200
00:14:53,840 --> 00:14:55,360
Can you tell me a little bit about that?

201
00:14:56,020 --> 00:14:56,320
Sure.

202
00:14:56,320 --> 00:14:56,640
Sure.

203
00:14:56,700 --> 00:15:04,400
Well, when I arrived in Iraq, the scouting program over there had just started back up because they were decertified.

204
00:15:04,960 --> 00:15:07,060
And so they were trying to stand them back up.

205
00:15:07,160 --> 00:15:10,940
So they set some councils up under the Ministry of Education.

206
00:15:10,940 --> 00:15:17,080
And scouts on post, the Iraqi Special Operations Forces, sponsored a troop.

207
00:15:17,220 --> 00:15:17,900
So that was a troop.

208
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:19,660
That was the Victory Base Council.

209
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:26,280
Every Saturday, we would drive out to the scouting area, you know, do events for the kids.

210
00:15:26,500 --> 00:15:28,720
I helped teach archery while I was there.

211
00:15:28,780 --> 00:15:29,760
I helped with chess.

212
00:15:29,760 --> 00:15:32,380
But every Saturday, they would do an event.

213
00:15:33,020 --> 00:15:37,260
The Iraqi Special Operations Forces were great because they were the sponsors.

214
00:15:37,680 --> 00:15:40,540
But it was just a great experience.

215
00:15:40,540 --> 00:15:51,880
And we wanted to make sure that those kids saw things and got opportunities that we have.

216
00:15:52,080 --> 00:15:54,680
And a lot of Iraqi kids were not privileged to.

217
00:15:54,680 --> 00:16:10,020
And it was important for us in the scouting program over there to show those kids who have seen violence most of their lives, the human side of us as soldiers.

218
00:16:11,140 --> 00:16:22,920
To see U.S. soldiers, you know, lay down their weapons and go forward to help them and teach and assist and just see the human side.

219
00:16:22,920 --> 00:16:25,800
But it really, I think it's really going to make a difference.

220
00:16:25,880 --> 00:16:33,120
And we sort of looked at it, too, is we're hopefully making a positive impact on the future leaders of Iraq.

221
00:16:33,360 --> 00:16:45,079
You know, they will say that the Americans were good people and they came in and they just, you know, helped us.

222
00:16:45,080 --> 00:16:53,580
And that's what we're looking at, hopefully, the impact that we will make on those young scouters, girls and boys there.

223
00:16:53,940 --> 00:16:55,980
It's very hard for us.

224
00:16:56,240 --> 00:17:08,280
Well, I'll say it's very hard for me living in relative security and comfort most of my life to picture what it would be like to be in a war zone, much less to be a child there.

225
00:17:08,280 --> 00:17:13,940
You know, the services and things that we take for granted, they may not have.

226
00:17:14,540 --> 00:17:27,180
You know, we were talking one day and with one of the Iraqi interpreters, I was listening to his frustration with, you know, hey, I need to get my daughter to the to the medical facility.

227
00:17:27,180 --> 00:17:33,200
And, you know, I can't move around in a vehicle like I need to and those type of things.

228
00:17:33,200 --> 00:17:43,740
So the work that you did and the attitude of the children, how how would it have been all that much different had you been working with your group of scouts there at Fort Meade?

229
00:17:43,860 --> 00:17:48,960
You know what? The beautiful thing about kids around the world, kids are kids.

230
00:17:50,040 --> 00:17:51,800
Kids understand fun.

231
00:17:52,500 --> 00:17:53,980
They had fun.

232
00:17:54,100 --> 00:17:58,640
And the same thing our scouts do over here, they did there.

233
00:17:58,640 --> 00:18:01,580
I mean, you had some you had some little rascals.

234
00:18:01,580 --> 00:18:11,639
What I've learned from experiencing 22 countries in the world is that people are people.

235
00:18:11,919 --> 00:18:15,300
If you talk to the average person, they just want love, security.

236
00:18:15,820 --> 00:18:18,020
You know, I'm saying to be able to take care of themselves.

237
00:18:18,460 --> 00:18:19,540
That's the average person.

238
00:18:19,980 --> 00:18:26,000
The average person just wants to be able to have safety, security and love at home.

239
00:18:26,280 --> 00:18:27,000
The average person.

240
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:30,460
So 21 years of service and you're getting ready to retire.

241
00:18:30,560 --> 00:18:31,439
Any thoughts about that?

242
00:18:31,580 --> 00:18:32,100
It's been great.

243
00:18:32,220 --> 00:18:36,480
I mean, I can look back fondly over the military and say it was great.

244
00:18:36,679 --> 00:18:38,879
I'm glad I did it.

245
00:18:39,280 --> 00:18:43,520
And I'm looking forward to, you know, continuing in service.

246
00:18:43,520 --> 00:18:52,060
So thinking back to Mr. Sexton, your scout master and the things that you've worked with and been able to do as a scout leader.

247
00:18:52,560 --> 00:18:57,860
If you were given the opportunity to say some generally encouraging things to scout leaders, what would you?

248
00:18:57,860 --> 00:18:58,520
I will tell you.

249
00:18:58,620 --> 00:19:04,620
And I mean, I know this is a this is a tough and sensitive area, but I'm just being sincere with you.

250
00:19:04,620 --> 00:19:08,179
Race was a big issue when I was there.

251
00:19:08,239 --> 00:19:13,899
I was the first African-American to make Eagle Scout in the Northeast Georgia Council.

252
00:19:13,900 --> 00:19:18,900
And there were not a lot of African-American shouting in my town.

253
00:19:18,900 --> 00:19:32,180
But again, as I moved closer to earning it, there were people that, you know, that can't, you know, shouldn't happen and things like that.

254
00:19:32,180 --> 00:19:35,560
But Mr. Sexton took a stand.

255
00:19:35,940 --> 00:19:37,700
You know, these are young men.

256
00:19:38,300 --> 00:19:39,500
I'm not into race.

257
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:44,840
I'm into providing the opportunities and development to these young men.

258
00:19:44,840 --> 00:19:45,760
What happened there?

259
00:19:45,760 --> 00:19:50,940
I mean, I can't imagine going along and you're doing good and things are working out.

260
00:19:51,020 --> 00:19:53,540
And then all of a sudden somebody hits you with something like that.

261
00:19:53,540 --> 00:19:57,560
I hold no heart feelings or anything like that.

262
00:19:57,560 --> 00:20:04,280
But I mean, like a senior patrolling saying it will be over his dead body before a N becomes a senior patrolling here.

263
00:20:04,720 --> 00:20:09,240
I addressed that with the scoutmaster and he did, you know, talk to one.

264
00:20:09,399 --> 00:20:11,440
And I got good support.

265
00:20:11,440 --> 00:20:15,740
But, you know, some of the key people that should have been supporting did not.

266
00:20:15,899 --> 00:20:22,340
But that wasn't a problem to me because the focus was earning it and then, you know, and proceeding on.

267
00:20:22,480 --> 00:20:23,500
And that worked out.

268
00:20:23,500 --> 00:20:39,200
And I think that scoutmasters need to be situationally aware of racism, classism, because our boys, they mimic or they model after those leaders that influence them.

269
00:20:39,300 --> 00:20:41,940
Scoutmasters have a lot of influence.

270
00:20:41,940 --> 00:20:46,040
And I'll tell you, I'll even get more specific for scoutmasters.

271
00:20:46,200 --> 00:20:47,660
Take politics.

272
00:20:48,020 --> 00:20:53,560
I think they need to be situationally aware of when they're around the boys, the messages that they are sending.

273
00:20:54,080 --> 00:20:56,000
And that's so important.

274
00:20:56,180 --> 00:21:02,200
And like I said, America is a beautiful place and we have the right to vote and express our opinions.

275
00:21:02,200 --> 00:21:19,940
But, again, as adults and scoutmasters and assistant scoutmasters, you never know what a young man is thinking or how you are influencing his mind or ideas about things based upon your conversations or your experiences.

276
00:21:20,180 --> 00:21:22,880
So you've got to be very mindful of that, I think, as leaders.

277
00:21:23,620 --> 00:21:29,520
We're always encouraged to be nonsectarian in matters of religion and politics.

278
00:21:29,520 --> 00:21:48,379
But you're telling me that as an African-American young man, that the thing that influenced your ability to succeed and to achieve was somebody who believed in you for no other reason than you had the drive to be able to succeed.

279
00:21:48,380 --> 00:21:52,840
One of the things I haven't told you, also, I'm actually oldest of seven.

280
00:21:53,660 --> 00:21:56,400
And my father left home when I was 13.

281
00:21:56,740 --> 00:21:58,140
Left my mom with seven kids.

282
00:21:58,820 --> 00:22:05,000
And of that seven, two eagles and one life and one star.

283
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:11,160
So that was a big part of not only your life, but the life of some of your brothers.

284
00:22:11,700 --> 00:22:16,380
And so a big part of your family from that little scout troop and from Mr.

285
00:22:16,480 --> 00:22:30,100
Sexton and your family to the White House and to being in Iraq and having these tremendous responsibilities and being able to serve your country for the past 20 years.

286
00:22:30,100 --> 00:22:31,379
I feel so privileged.

287
00:22:31,379 --> 00:22:34,340
I tell people I feel so privileged to be an American.

288
00:22:34,679 --> 00:22:37,060
You can do and be anything you want to in America.

289
00:22:37,560 --> 00:22:39,419
And our scouts need to know that.

290
00:22:39,840 --> 00:22:42,260
And scouting, I think, instills that.

291
00:22:42,260 --> 00:22:55,520
So the message is, I'll borrow one of Dr. King's lines, that the content of a person's character is the most important part of any consideration we should make in relating to them.

292
00:22:55,580 --> 00:22:57,300
That is absolutely correct.

293
00:22:57,300 --> 00:23:01,520
Well, in a career over 20 years, and how many countries did you say?

294
00:23:01,620 --> 00:23:02,620
Did I hear you say 22?

295
00:23:02,620 --> 00:23:02,879
22.

296
00:23:03,180 --> 00:23:03,540
Seven?

297
00:23:03,919 --> 00:23:04,899
22 countries.

298
00:23:05,419 --> 00:23:16,659
Those universal things that you learned as becoming an Eagle Scout and a youth leader in your troop, you found have had pretty good application just about anywhere you go.

299
00:23:16,820 --> 00:23:17,320
Absolutely.

300
00:23:17,659 --> 00:23:17,980
Thank you.

301
00:23:17,980 --> 00:23:18,760
No, no.

302
00:23:18,899 --> 00:23:20,060
A scout is trustworthy.

303
00:23:20,060 --> 00:23:27,700
You know, and working with an interpreter, you know, saying, OK, here's what we will do.

304
00:23:28,340 --> 00:23:30,080
Here's what we need you to do.

305
00:23:30,379 --> 00:23:32,720
And if you do this, we're going to come through on our part.

306
00:23:32,899 --> 00:23:39,760
And I got word back, you know, after a couple of months working with him, hey, we like Thornton because he says what he's going to do and he's going to do it.

307
00:23:39,760 --> 00:23:50,000
You know, kind, you know, courteous, all those things and living and dealing with people really makes a difference and really propels you forward.

308
00:23:50,200 --> 00:23:56,320
And, you know, back to the Scoutmasters, they have to live that because that's what the Scouts see.

309
00:23:56,320 --> 00:24:05,439
And not taking away tough love, because Mr. Sexton, he demonstrates some tough love with me, but I appreciate it.

310
00:24:06,320 --> 00:24:09,540
Yeah, every once in a while, that's the job a Scoutmaster needs to do.

311
00:24:09,840 --> 00:24:17,240
It certainly has been an honor to be able to speak with you and wish you continued success in your new role at Morehouse College.

312
00:24:17,240 --> 00:24:25,020
I appreciate what you're doing, you know, with the podcasting and getting the message out because that is so important.

313
00:24:25,020 --> 00:24:26,720
All right. Thank you so much, sir.

314
00:24:26,720 --> 00:24:27,300
Okay. Bye-bye.

315
00:24:41,300 --> 00:24:43,379
Thanks for listening. We went a little over time.

316
00:24:44,040 --> 00:24:48,300
So I owe you the second installment on Avoiding Eagle Scout Drama.

317
00:24:48,639 --> 00:24:51,220
That'll be in the next podcast, podcast number 13.

318
00:24:51,220 --> 00:25:02,100
You can contact me, Clark Green, at ClarkGreen, C-L-A-R-K-E-G-R-E-E-N, at gmail.com.

319
00:25:03,020 --> 00:25:07,900
The Scoutmaster blog and the Scoutmaster podcast are not official publications of the Boy Scouts of America,

320
00:25:08,040 --> 00:25:11,840
nor are they endorsed or sponsored by the Boy Scouts of America.

321
00:25:12,380 --> 00:25:17,440
They're just a way to try and lend a hand to Scout leaders and perhaps have a bit of fun along the way.

322
00:25:17,440 --> 00:25:23,940
Follow us on Facebook at Scoutmaster blog and Scoutmaster blog on Twitter.

323
00:25:25,340 --> 00:25:30,660
And so, Robert, can you say something for the nice folks?

324
00:25:30,740 --> 00:25:32,820
Good luck to you and good camping.

325
00:25:33,100 --> 00:25:34,020
Well, thank you very much.

326
00:25:34,460 --> 00:25:35,760
We'll talk to you next time.

327
00:25:35,760 --> 00:26:05,740
Thank you.

328
00:26:05,760 --> 00:26:07,560
Thank you.

329
00:26:07,560 --> 00:26:33,940
Thank you very much.

